<https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency#graph> {
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Have I actually said the thing I am assuming they already picked up from my tone or face?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/1> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "In this email, would a reader who could not hear my voice know whether I was joking, annoyed or serious?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/2> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Did I check what they understood, or did I take their nod as proof?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/3> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Before this presentation, am I treating my racing heart as something the audience can see?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency/check/4> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-11-03"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#Barrier> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Illusion of transparency" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "overestimating how obvious we are" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "transparency illusion" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/egocentric-bias> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The tendency to overestimate how clearly other people can see our internal states - our nerves, feelings and intentions - and, by extension, how plainly our messages convey what we meant, so we under-signal and fail to check understanding." .
