<https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback#graph> {
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "If I strip out the softeners or intensifiers, what is the core message I am sending - and would this listener decode it the same way?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback/check/1> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Am I about to deliver criticism in front of people who do not need to hear it?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback/check/2> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Have I checked what the person understood, rather than assuming my politeness or bluntness landed as intended?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback/check/3> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-11-03"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#Concept> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Direct vs. indirect feedback" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "direct and indirect negative feedback" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "evaluating scale" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "feedback directness" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "upgraders and downgraders" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/feedback> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/intercultural-communication> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The contrast between stating criticism plainly, often intensified with \"upgraders\" such as \"totally\", and softening it with \"downgraders\", understatement or omission so the listener infers the problem; norms differ across cultures, but research shows the gap shifts with setting, status and urgency rather than following nationality alone." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/scheme/cross-cultural-communication> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/direct-vs-indirect-feedback> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Direct vs. indirect feedback" .
