  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Have I put the number next to the word, or am I relying on readers to look up a key they will probably skip?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/2> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Am I using \"could\", \"may\" or \"possible\" to hedge when I actually have an estimate I am reluctant to commit to?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/3> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Have I kept my confidence in the evidence separate from the likelihood of the event?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/4> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/example> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#Rewrite> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#after> "We judge it unlikely (20-45%) that we hit the March 1 go-live; very likely (80-95%) by March 22. We have moderate confidence: the estimate rests on two weeks of test-defect data. Decision needed by Friday: hold the date with reduced scope, or move it." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#before> "There is a real possibility we could miss the March 1 go-live, although the team is working hard and it may well still be achievable." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#situation> "A consultant updates a client steering committee on whether a system migration will hit its go-live date." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#why> "The before version could be read anywhere from a small risk to a probable slip; the after version gives one shared range, separates odds from confidence, and turns the forecast into a decision the committee can make." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/1> <https://schema.org/position> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/1> <https://schema.org/text> "Agree a short likelihood scale with your team or client before you need it - for example remote (under 5%), unlikely (20-45%), roughly even (45-55%), likely (55-80%), very likely (80-95%) - with ranges that do not overlap." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/2> <https://schema.org/position> "2"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/2> <https://schema.org/text> "Before writing a forecast, decide your own number or range first, then pick the one word on the scale whose range contains it." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/3> <https://schema.org/position> "3"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/3> <https://schema.org/text> "Put the numeric range in brackets right after the word in the text itself (\"likely (55-80%)\"), not in a footnote, appendix or hover text." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/4> <https://schema.org/position> "4"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/4> <https://schema.org/text> "Keep likelihood (how probable the event is) separate from confidence (how solid your evidence is), and say each in its own sentence." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/5> <https://schema.org/position> "5"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/5> <https://schema.org/text> "Reserve \"possible\", \"could\" and \"may\" for cases where you genuinely cannot estimate the odds, and say so rather than using them as softeners." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/6> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/6> <https://schema.org/position> "6"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/6> <https://schema.org/text> "Use the same words with the same meanings in every report, and add a short key whenever a new reader joins the distribution." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowTo> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto> <https://schema.org/name> "Words of estimative probability" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/1> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/2> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/3> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/4> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/5> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto> <https://schema.org/step> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/6> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/created> "2026-11-03"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#Technique> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Words of estimative probability" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "calibrated likelihood words" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "estimative language" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "probability lexicon" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "verbal probability expressions" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/communicating-uncertainty> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Stating how likely something is with an agreed set of likelihood words, each tied to a numeric range that is shown next to the word, so that \"likely\" means the same thing to the writer and every reader." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/scheme/executive-and-client-communication> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Words of estimative probability" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/bottom-line-up-front> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/executive-summary> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/illusion-of-transparency> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/plain-language> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/source/words-of-estimative-probability/s1> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/source/words-of-estimative-probability/s2> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/source/words-of-estimative-probability/s3> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#wasDerivedFrom> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/source/words-of-estimative-probability/s4> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#addresses> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/vague-probability-language> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#appliedIn> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/executive-briefing> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#demonstratedBy> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/wintle-et-al-2019> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#domain> "executive-and-client-communication" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#evidenceStatus> "mixed" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/example> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#file> "articles/executive-and-client-communication/words-of-estimative-probability.md" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#howTo> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#introducedBy> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/sherman-kent> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#level> "intermediate" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#supportedBy> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/mandel-irwin-2021> .
}

<https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact#graph> {
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/1> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Could the other person picture the exact moment I mean, or did I say something vague like \"lately\"?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/1> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/2> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Is my behaviour description something a camera could record, or did I slip in a judgement or guess at a motive?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/2> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/3> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Have I said what the impact actually was, rather than assuming they already know?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/3> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#SelfCheckQuestion> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/4> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Did I leave room to ask about their intent and listen before deciding what should change?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/check/4> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#questionFor> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/example> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#Rewrite> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#after> "In this morning's quarterly review with the VPs, when Priya asked where the churn figure came from, you said 'I'd have to check' and moved on. The VPs then questioned two other numbers and we lost ten minutes of the agenda, and I was worried they'd discount the whole forecast. What was going on for you at that point?" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#before> "You came across as really unprepared today and kind of defensive. You need to step up your game before the next one." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#situation> "A team lead gives feedback to an analyst who presented the quarterly numbers at a leadership review." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/example> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#why> "The rewrite swaps verdicts (\"unprepared\", \"defensive\") for a time-stamped situation, an observable behaviour and a concrete impact, then asks about intent - so the analyst can see exactly what to change and has room to explain instead of defending their character." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/1> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/1> <https://schema.org/position> "1"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/1> <https://schema.org/text> "Pin down the situation - the specific meeting, document or moment, with a time and place (\"in Tuesday's 10 a.m. client call\"), not \"lately\" or \"last week\"." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/2> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/2> <https://schema.org/position> "2"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/2> <https://schema.org/text> "Describe the behaviour as something a camera could have recorded - words said, actions taken - and strip out labels such as \"rude\", \"lazy\" or \"great attitude\"." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/3> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/3> <https://schema.org/position> "3"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/3> <https://schema.org/text> "State the impact the behaviour had on you, the team or the work, in your own voice, naming a concrete consequence and, if useful, your reaction (frustrated, relieved, proud)." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/4> <https://schema.org/position> "4"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/4> <https://schema.org/text> "For redirecting feedback, stop and ask about intent (\"What were you aiming for there?\") and listen before proposing any fix." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/5> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/5> <https://schema.org/position> "5"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/5> <https://schema.org/text> "Agree together on what to keep doing or what to try next time, and give the feedback close to the event while it is still fresh." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/6> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowToStep> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/6> <https://schema.org/position> "6"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto/6> <https://schema.org/text> "Use the same three parts for positive feedback, so the person knows exactly which behaviour to repeat." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://schema.org/HowTo> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/situation-behavior-impact/howto> <https://schema.org/name> "Situation-Behavior-Impact" .
