  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/open-ended-questions> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/ontology#Technique> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/open-ended-questions> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Open-ended questions" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/open-ended-questions> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "invitational questions" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/open-ended-questions> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "open questions" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/open-ended-questions> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "open-ended questioning" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/open-ended-questions> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/questioning> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/open-ended-questions> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Questions that cannot be answered with \"yes\", \"no\" or a single fact - typically starting with \"what\" or \"how\" - asked to let the other person explain in their own words, so you learn what they know, think and need rather than what you assumed." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/open-ended-questions> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/scheme/listening> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/open-ended-questions> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "Open-ended questions" .
